WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit nine: Constitutional Theory. Today you will argue about government using prevent / protect against + noun.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.260
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.456
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:25.771
Constitutionalism.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:31.987
Constitutionalism means the principle that government power is limited by a constitution.

8
00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:36.324
Constitutionalism keeps even majorities within legal limits.

9
00:00:36.917 --> 00:00:40.103
Your turn. Say: Constitutionalism.

10
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:44.455
Constitutionalism.

11
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.019
Separation of powers.

12
00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:54.161
Separation of powers means dividing government into legislative, executive and judicial branches.

13
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.572
Separation of powers stops any one branch from dominating.

14
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:02.579
Your turn. Say: Separation of powers.

15
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:07.177
Separation of powers.

16
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:09.173
Judicial review.

17
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:14.772
Judicial review means the power of courts to strike down unconstitutional laws.

18
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:18.370
Judicial review enabled the court to cancel the statute.

19
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:22.112
Your turn. Say: Judicial review.

20
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:26.258
Judicial review.

21
00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:28.540
Checks and balances.

22
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.488
Checks and balances means mechanisms by which branches limit one another.

23
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:37.464
Checks and balances require cooperation between branches.

24
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.608
Your turn. Say: Checks and balances.

25
00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:46.292
Checks and balances.

26
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.632
Amendment process.

27
00:01:49.617 --> 00:01:53.439
Amendment process means the formal procedure for changing a constitution.

28
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.909
A demanding amendment process protects stability.

29
00:01:57.517 --> 00:02:00.726
Your turn. Say: Amendment process.

30
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.218
Amendment process.

31
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:06.996
Federalism.

32
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:12.874
Federalism means a system dividing power between central and regional governments.

33
00:02:13.483 --> 00:02:16.307
Federalism lets states experiment with policy.

34
00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:19.465
Your turn. Say: Federalism.

35
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.464
Federalism.

36
00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:25.450
Civil liberties.

37
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.981
Civil liberties means basic freedoms protected from government interference.

38
00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.303
Civil liberties include speech and assembly.

39
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:37.952
Your turn. Say: Civil liberties.

40
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.242
Civil liberties.

41
00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:44.948
Constitutional interpretation.

42
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.736
Constitutional interpretation means the process of deciding what a constitution means.

43
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.066
Constitutional interpretation divides originalists from living-document theorists.

44
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:00.452
Your turn. Say: Constitutional interpretation.

45
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.578
Constitutional interpretation.

46
00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:10.366
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:11.217 --> 00:03:14.633
Visal, why can't a government just do what the majority wants?

48
00:03:15.717 --> 00:03:22.769
Because checks and balances prevent the abuse of power. Even a majority can threaten minorities.

49
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:26.103
How do they protect people in practice?

50
00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:37.900
Judicial review protects civil liberties against hasty legislation, and the separation of powers guards against concentration of authority.

51
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:40.354
Isn't that slow?

52
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:50.337
Deliberately so. Slowness helps prevent rash decisions, and a demanding amendment process protects against casual change.

53
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.476
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:58.058
Grammar focus: Prevent and protect against plus noun.

55
00:03:58.358 --> 00:03:59.579
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:00.283 --> 00:04:03.084
Checks and balances prevent the abuse of power.

57
00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:08.247
Judicial review protects civil liberties against hasty legislation.

58
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.450
A strict amendment process guards against casual change.

59
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:26.132
Prevent + noun (no preposition): prevent abuse. Protect/guard + noun + against + threat: protect liberties against abuse. 'Prevent from + -ing' needs a person: prevent them from acting.

60
00:04:26.883 --> 00:04:30.801
Be careful! Don't say: Checks prevents abuse of power.

61
00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:35.254
Say: Checks prevent the abuse of power.

62
00:04:35.554 --> 00:04:37.565
Plural subject, plural verb.

63
00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:41.298
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.426
Limiting Power.

65
00:04:43.726 --> 00:04:48.454
Constitutionalism holds that government must be bound by rules it cannot change at will.

66
00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:57.631
The separation of powers divides authority among legislative, executive and judicial branches, so that checks and balances prevent the abuse of power.

67
00:04:58.633 --> 00:05:05.637
Judicial review protects civil liberties against majority rule, since courts may strike down laws that violate the constitution.

68
00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:11.431
Federalism adds a vertical check by dividing power between central and regional governments.

69
00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:19.460
A demanding amendment process guards against casual change, though critics argue that it can also preserve outdated provisions.

70
00:05:20.567 --> 00:05:24.557
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:25.417 --> 00:05:28.113
Checks and balances blank the abuse of power.

72
00:05:28.413 --> 00:05:32.542
A, prevent. B, prevent from. C, prevent of.

73
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.041
The answer is A: prevent.

74
00:05:39.183 --> 00:05:42.994
The power of courts to strike down unconstitutional laws is blank.

75
00:05:43.294 --> 00:05:47.342
A, federalism. B, judicial review. C, amendment.

76
00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.352
The answer is B: judicial review.

77
00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:57.967
Dividing power between central and regional governments is blank.

78
00:05:58.267 --> 00:06:02.617
A, federalism. B, constitutionalism. C, separation.

79
00:06:06.167 --> 00:06:08.410
The answer is A: federalism.

80
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.352
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:11.652 --> 00:06:19.532
A, Courts protect liberties against abuse.. B, Courts protect liberties of abuse.. C, Courts protect liberties abuse..

82
00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:26.773
The answer is A: Courts protect liberties against abuse..

83
00:06:27.917 --> 00:06:31.797
According to the reading, critics argue that amendment processes can…

84
00:06:32.097 --> 00:06:37.260
A, preserve outdated provisions. B, speed reform. C, end review.

85
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.810
The answer is A: preserve outdated provisions.

86
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.082
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:48.917 --> 00:06:52.275
Why are checks and balances important in a system of government?

88
00:07:09.083 --> 00:07:10.584
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:10.884 --> 00:07:36.506
Checks and balances are important because power tends to concentrate, and concentrated power is easily abused. When each branch can limit the others, no single group can rule unchecked. Courts protect civil liberties against the majority, and legislatures can restrain executives. The system is slower, but it prevents rash and unfair decisions.

90
00:07:36.806 --> 00:07:38.562
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:44.018
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and prevent and protect against plus noun.

92
00:07:44.318 --> 00:07:49.908
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

93
00:07:50.208 --> 00:07:52.744
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:53.044 --> 00:07:55.789
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

